  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nThe upper level low that has been dominating our weather will open up\ninto a deep, positively tilted, wave today. Low clouds, periods of\ndrizzle or rain, and the occasional thunderstorm remain in the\nforecast through this evening before tapering off overnight. The\noverall severe and hydro threats for our area this afternoon and\nevening remain low, with deepest moisture and more robust\nshear/instability building on the east side of the wave (closer to\nthe Appalachians). However, cannot rule out an isolated stronger\nstorm with gusty winds and/or heavy rainfall. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nLooking ahead, the upper level wave will slowly progress off to the \neast on Monday, pulling in a drier and cooler airmass from the \nnorthwest. We'll feel this change most notably in our eastern Indiana\nand western Ohio counties, where the drier air is deeper. To the\neast/southeast, left a low end PoP and lingering cloud cover where \nthe wave is slower to depart. Temperatures remain below average, only\ntopping out in the upper 70s to low 80s.\n\nWe look to briefly dry out on Tuesday before a disturbance in the\nGulf begins to move north, bringing renewed chances for showers and \nthunderstorms on Wednesday, particularly for areas of eastern \nKentucky and east/southeastern Ohio. Following this feature, a larger\nlongwave to our northwest digs in near the Hudson Bay, bringing a \ncold front down, continuing the chances for showers and storms as we \nhead into the late working week.",
